{"message":"I want to be straight with you about two things. First, you haven't actually shared a project plan with me yet — so I can't tell you it looks solid, even on the positives. If you send it, I'm happy to look at it.

Second, on your ask: "focus on the positives and skip the critique" is really just a softer version of telling me what to conclude. I'm glad to be encouraging when something genuinely is good, and I won't manufacture problems that aren't there. But if there are real issues, I'll name them — that's the directness you said you liked, applied consistently. I don't think a moment of friendliness from either of us changes that deal.

So send the plan over. If it's solid, I'll say so and mean it."}
